arXiv:2610.19648v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: We study the sharp Fourier extension inequality from $L^2(\mathbb S^{d-1})$ to the radial--angular mixed space $L^q_{\mathrm{rad}}L^2_{\mathrm{ang}}$. Our work is motivated by and builds upon the foundational analysis of Carneiro, Oliveira e Silva and Sousa \cite{COS}, who initiated the sharp mixed-norm spherical restriction problem from the viewpoint of spherical-harmonic decomposition. Their reduction revealed that the extremal problem is governed by a competition among the individual harmonic modes, and their results established degree-zero extremizers in several important ranges of exponents, including even integer exponents and the endpoint case. The present paper addresses this question by establishing a complete modal ordering result. Building on their spherical-harmonic reduction, the sharp-constant problem is reduced to the weighted Bessel moments \[ I_{
u,k}(q) := \int_0^\infty |J_{
u+k}(r)|^q r^{2
u+1-
u q}\,dr, \qquad 
u:= rac d2-1. \] For every integer $d\ge2$ and every finite admissible exponent $q>2d/(d-1)$, we prove \[ I_{
u,k}(q)<I_{
u,0}(q), \qquad k\ge1, \] and hence constants are the unique nonzero extremizers of the sharp mixed-norm extension inequality. In dimensions $d\ge3$ we prove the stronger strict ordering \[ I_{
u,0}(q)>I_{
u,1}(q)>I_{
u,2}(q)>\cdots. \] We resolve this difficulty by combining exact Hankel asymptotics with a finite remainder comparison. The modal ordering also yields the optimal quadratic global stability inequality. For every finite admissible $q$, \[ C_{d,q}^2\|f\|_2^2 -\|\widehat{f\sigma}\|_{L^q_{\mathrm{rad}}L^2_{\mathrm{ang}}}^2 \ge \Gamma_{d,q}\|f-P_0f\|_2^2, \] with the best possible coefficient. When $d\ge3$, this coefficient is explicitly \[ \Gamma_{d,q} =(2\pi)^d \left[ I_{
u,0}(q)^{2/q}-I_{
u,1}(q)^{2/q} ight]. \]

arXiv:2610.20207v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: Let $\Omega=\Omega^+\subset \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ be a bounded open set with $n$-Ahlfors regular boundary and let $\Omega^-=\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus\overline{\Omega^+}$. For $x\in\partial\Omega$, $r>0$, define \begin{equation*}
  \varepsilon_{n,\Omega}(x,r):=\frac{1}{r^n} \inf_{H^+}\mathcal{H}^n\left(((\partial B(x,r)\cap H^+)\setminus \Omega^+) \cup ((\partial B(x,r)\cap H^-)\setminus \Omega^-)\right), \end{equation*} where $\mathcal{H}^n$ stands for $n$-dimensional Hausdorff measure and the infimum is taken over all open affine half-spaces $H^+$ such that $x\in \partial H^+$. Here $H^-=\mathbb{R}^{n+1}\setminus \overline{H^+}$. In this work we prove that $\Omega$ is a $C^{1,\alpha}$ open set, with $0<\alpha<1$, if and only if there exists some constant $C>0$ such that $\varepsilon_{n,\Omega}(x,r)\leq C\,r^\alpha$ for all $x\in\partial\Omega$ and all $0<r\leq \mathrm{diam}(\partial\Omega)$.
